<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ndatte Yalla Mbodj</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1810–1860</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ndatte Yalla Mbodj was the last ruling queen (linger) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waalo">Waalo</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, holding power from 1846. She resisted French expansion from Saint-Louis and raids by the Trarza Moors, and sought to control trade on the lower Senegal river. In 1855 French forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Louis Faidherbe</a> invaded and annexed Waalo. She is remembered in Senegal as a symbol of resistance.</p></div>
